","mTOR inhibitors are a class of drugs that target the mechanistic mammalian activator of protein kinase C (mTOR) pathway. mTOR is a serine kinase that plays a crucial role in cell growth, proliferation, differentiation, and survival. It is activated by various signals, including growth factors, and is essential for maintaining cellular homeostasis.

mTOR inhibitors are used in cancer treatment and other diseases where mTOR signaling is overregulated. mTOR inhibitors are classified into two main classes: mTOR-CII inhibitors and mTOR-CII/CII inhibitors. mTOR is part of two complexes, mTOR-C1 and mTOR-C2, which have distinct functions and are activated by different pathways.

mTOR inhibitors work by binding to and inhibiting the activity of mTOR, thereby reducing the activity of its downstream kinases and proteins involved in the mTOR pathway. This leads to a decrease in the activity of the mTOR pathway, which in turn can inhibit cell growth, proliferation, and survival.

Inhibitors of mTOR-C1 (e.g., Sunitinib, Sorapatinib, Pazofatinib, and Pazofibib) are more common and have been used in cancer treatment. These inhibitors target the PI3K/Akt/mTOR signaling pathway, which is often overactivated in cancer cells. By inhibiting mTOR-C1, these inhibitors can inhibit the activity of downstream kinases and proteins involved in the MAPK pathway, which is involved in cell growth and proliferation.

Inhibitors of mTOR-C2 (e.g., MLK-1) are less common and have been less studied. These inhibitors target the PI3K/Akt/mTOR signaling pathway, which is also involved in cell growth and proliferation. By inhibiting mTOR-C2, these inhibitors can inhibit the activity of downstream kinases and proteins involved in the MAPK pathway, which is involved in cell growth and proliferation.

