Chronic stress can lead to a decrease in NK cell activity, which can increase the risk of infections, cancer, and other diseases. NK cell activity is affected by NK cell count, NK cell function, and NK cell migration. NK cell count is reduced in response to chronic stress, while NK cell function and migration are also negatively affected.

The primary mechanisms behind the suppression of NK cell function in response to chronic stress include:

1. Cytokine dysregulation: NK cell function is influenced by a variety of cytokines, such as TNF-alpha, IL-12, and IL-18, which promote their activation and activation of other immune cells. Chronic stress can lead to an imbalance in these cytokines, reducing the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines and increasing the production of anti-inflammatory cytokines, such as IL-11 and TGF-beta. This imbalance can dampen NK cell activity.

