5. Prooxytocin-12 (POMS): The release of oxytocin during stress can lead to the production of POMS, which can inhibit NK cell activity.

In summary, chronic stress can lead to a decrease in NK cell activity through multiple mechanisms, including cytokine dysregulation, CCL12-induced migration of NK cells to the brain, IL-11 production, prooxytocin, and POMS. These mechanisms can lead to a decrease in the host's ability to fight infections and cancer, making them more susceptible to these diseases.",relevance,"# Task Description
