</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we need to use the standard molar entropy values (S°) of the reactants and products. The balanced chemical equation for the reaction is:

CO(g) + 1/2 O2(g) → CO2(g)

Since we are burning 3 moles of CO, we can multiply the equation by 3:

3 CO(g) + 3/2 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

Now, we can use the standard molar entropy values (S°) at 298 K:

S°(CO) = 135.6 J/mol·K
S°(O2) = 205.0 J/mol·K
S°(CO2) = 214.0 J/mol·K

Now, we can calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) using the formula:

ΔS = Σ [S°(products)] - Σ [S°(reactants)]

ΔS = [3 * S°(CO2)] - [3 * S°(CO) + 3/2 * S°(O2)]

ΔS = [3 * 214.0 J/mol·K] - [3 * 135.6 J/mol·K + 3/2 * 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 642.0 J/mol·K - [406.8 J/mol·K + 307.5 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 642.0 J/mol·K - 714.3 J/mol·K

ΔS = -72.3 J/mol·K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -72.3 J/mol·K.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

