","(1) Simplify the expression $\dfrac{\cos (180 ^{\circ} + \alpha) \cdot \sin (\alpha + 360 ^{\circ})}{\sin (-\alpha - 180 ^{\circ}) \cdot \cos (-180^{\circ} - \alpha)}$.

Using the identities $\cos (180^\circ + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, $\sin (\alpha + 360^\circ) = \sin \alpha$, $\sin (-\alpha - 180^\circ) = \sin (\alpha + 180^\circ) = -\sin \alpha$, and $\cos (-180^\circ - \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, the expression becomes:
\[
\dfrac{(-\cos \alpha) \cdot \sin \alpha}{(-\sin \alpha) \cdot (-\cos \alpha)} = \dfrac{-\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha}{\sin \alpha \cdot \cos \alpha} = -1.
