Using the identities $\cos (180^\circ + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, $\sin (\alpha + 360^\circ) = \sin \alpha$, $\sin (-\alpha - 180^\circ) = \sin (\alpha + 180^\circ) = -\sin \alpha$, and $\cos (-180^\circ - \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, the expression becomes:
\[
\dfrac{(-\cos \alpha) \cdot \sin \alpha}{(-\sin \alpha) \cdot (-\cos \alpha)} = \dfrac{-\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha}{\sin \alpha \cdot \cos \alpha} = -1.
\]

(2) Given $\tan \alpha = -\dfrac{3}{4}$, find the value of $\dfrac{\cos (\frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha) \cdot \sin (-\pi - \alpha)}{\cos (\frac{11 \pi}{2} - \alpha) \cdot \sin (\frac{11 \pi}{2} + \alpha)}$.

Using the identities $\cos \left( \frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha \right) = -\sin \alpha$, $\sin (-\pi - \alpha) = -\sin (\pi + \alpha) = \sin \alpha$, $\cos \left( \frac{11 \pi}{2} - \alpha \right) = \cos \left( \frac{11 \pi}{2} - \alpha \right) = \cos \left( 5\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} - \alpha \right) = \cos \left( \pi + \frac{\pi}{2} - \alpha \right) = -\cos \left( \frac{\pi}{2} - \alpha \right) = -\cos \alpha$, and $\sin \left( \frac{11 \pi}{2} + \alpha \right) = \sin \left( 5\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha \right) = \sin \left( \pi + \frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha \right) = -\sin \left( \frac{\pi}{2} + \alpha \right) = --\sin \alpha = \sin \alpha$, the expression becomes:
\[
\frac{(-\sin \alpha) \cdot \sin \alpha}{(-\cos \alpha) \cdot \sin \alpha} = \frac{-\sin^2 \alpha}{-\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha} = \frac{\sin \alpha}{\cos \alpha} = \tan \alpha.
\]
