</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length (\(\lambda_d\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi \Delta n}
\]
where:
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the evanescent wave,
- \(\Delta n\) is the refractive difference between the material of the cladding and the medium of the evanescent wave.

Here, the cladding is Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{08}\)As, and the evanescent wave is in GaAs. The decay length is given by:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi (n_{\text{GaAs}} - n_{\text{Ga}_{0.2}Al_{0.8}As})}
\]
Given:
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu m = 1000 \, \mu m = 1000 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}\)
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the refractive index of the cladding (Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{_{0.8}}\)As) and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the evanescent wave (GaAs).

Thus, \(\Delta n = n_{\text{GaAs}} - n_{\text{Ga}_{0.2}Al_{0.8}As} = 0.3\).

Substituting the values:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{1000 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}}{2\pi (0.3)} = \frac{1000 \times 10^{-6}}{1.94} \, \text{m} \approx 516 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m} = 516 \, \mu m
\]

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{516 \, \mu m}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

