","The lifetime of a unstable particle is inversely proportional to its mass, as given by the Heisenberg uncertainty principle:  
\[
\Delta E \Delta t \approx \hbar
\]  
For a short-lived particle, the uncertainty in its rest energy (\(\Delta E\)) is inversely proportional to its lifetime (\(\Delta t\)). The uncertainty in the measured energy is given by:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar}{2\Delta t}
\]  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{1.055 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}}
\]  
Convert \(\hbar\) to MeV:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{(1.055 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}) \times (1 \text{ MeV}/1.602 \times 10^{13} \text{ J})}{4 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}}
\]  
\[
\Delta E \approx 6.6 \times 10^{-12} \text{ MeV}
\]  
Thus, the uncertainty in the measured rest energy is approximately \(6.6 \times 10^{-12} \text{ MeV}\).  

The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:  
\[
1385 \pm 6.6 \times 10^{-12} \text{ MeV}
\]  
This range is:  
\[
(1385 - 6.6 \times 10^{-12}) \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq (1385 + 6.6 \times 10^{-12}) \text{ MeV}
\]  
\[
1384.999999999 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1385.000000001 \text{ MeV}
\]  
The uncertainty is negligible compared to the rest energy, so the measured value would be very close to 1385 MeV.  

